Suprakernel Simulation Engine lab protocol

1. Purpose

Goal:Define a laboratory procedure for deploying, calibrating, and operating the Suprakernel Simulation Engine as a sovereign physics‑design system, using real astrophysical telemetry and enforcing inviolable constraints:

causality

conservation laws

ledger immutability

2. Required infrastructure

Compute cluster:

multi‑GPU / multi‑CPU nodes

low‑latency interconnect

Storage:

high‑reliability, append‑only ledger store (provenance spine)

Networking:

TLS 1.3‑secured internal network

Telemetry ingestion:

access to astrophysical datasets (e.g., surveys, time‑series, spectra)

Monitoring:

divergence monitors

resource and stability dashboards

3. Pre‑operation checks

3.1 Security and integrity

Verify TLS configuration between all engine components.

Enable dataset signature verification for all telemetry sources.

Confirm append‑only mode on provenance spine storage.

3.2 Suprakernel‑A initialization

Load hardware topology profile (cluster layout, routing).

Enable adaptive precision and kernel fusion modules.

Run a dry‑run execution test with trivial physics (e.g., static field).

3.3 Suprakernel‑B initialization

Load Astrophysical Tetrahedron state vector definition:(\Psi = {x, P, g, \phi, \sigma}) plus (\rho, v, B).

Configure Hamiltonian modifier ranges and warp metric bounds.

Enable chaos/volatility detection thresholds.

4. Operational procedure

Step 1 — Baseline manifold

Define a baseline physics manifold (e.g., standard GR + MHD).

Run Suprakernel‑B to generate initial state evolution.

Validate that outputs match known astrophysical behavior within tolerance.

Commit baseline to provenance spine as Genesis Manifold v0.

Step 2 — Telemetry ingestion (Observation Phase)

Connect to astrophysical telemetry streams or datasets.

Ingest data into the Observation Phase (The Mirror).

Compute divergence metrics between baseline manifold and real data.

Log divergence results to provenance spine.

Step 3 — Physics synthesis (Synthesis Phase)

Suprakernel‑B generates candidate physics configurations:

adjust Hamiltonians

modify warp metrics

tune potentials and curvature.

Suprakernel‑A optimizes execution architecture for these candidates.

Tag each candidate with a unique manifold ID and parameter set.

Step 4 — Twin validation (Validation Phase)

Run Twin simulations for each candidate manifold.

Compare simulated outputs against real telemetry:

spatial distributions

time evolution

spectral signatures

Reject candidates exceeding divergence thresholds.

Mark passing candidates as Validated Manifolds.

Step 5 — Resolution & commit (Resolution Phase)

Select best‑fit manifold(s) based on divergence, stability, and constraint adherence.

Verify causality and conservation laws for selected manifold.

Commit manifold and its full state evolution to the provenance spine.

Increment engine epoch (e.g., Suprakernel Epoch n+1).

Step 6 — Iterative refinement

Repeat Observation → Synthesis → Validation → Resolution.

Track manifold lineage in the provenance spine.

Periodically export epoch summaries for scientific analysis.

5. Safety and constraint enforcement

Causality:

disallow retrocausal dependencies in state evolution.

enforce time‑ordered commit semantics in the ledger.

Conservation laws:

monitor energy, momentum, charge, and mass conservation in all simulations.

automatically reject manifolds violating conservation beyond tolerance.

Ledger immutability:

no deletion or modification of committed entries.

all corrections recorded as new entries with explicit references.

6. Shutdown procedure

Gracefully stop new manifold generation in Suprakernel‑B.

Drain in‑flight simulations and finalize validation runs.

Flush all pending commits to the provenance spine.

Close telemetry ingestion channels.

Shut down Suprakernel‑A routing and release hardware resources.

Archive session metadata (epoch ID, manifolds, divergence logs).

